# Line endings. Every .sh here is executed by a POSIX shell, and on Windows that
# shell is Git Bash — which tolerates CRLF, but nothing else in the chain
# promises to: the same checkout run under WSL, a container, or a plain
# /bin/sh fails on a `\r` with errors that name the wrong thing entirely
# ("$'\r': command not found"). git's core.autocrlf=true, the Windows default,
# is what introduces them, so pin the files that must not have them.
*.sh            text eol=lf
*.py            text eol=lf
**/shims/*      text eol=lf

# Data the shell parses with sed/awk. A stray \r ends up INSIDE a captured
# value, where it survives every visual inspection: `mode: sabbath` parses to
# "sabbath\r", which matches no case pattern, and the pause switch silently
# stops pausing.
*.yaml          text eol=lf
*.yml           text eol=lf
.env.example    text eol=lf

# Documents. Agents read and rewrite these; keeping them LF keeps a Windows
# edit from showing up as a whole-file diff.
*.md            text eol=lf
